<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xavante</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as A'uwe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xavante are a Gê-speaking indigenous people of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In the 18th century they lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a>, where they clashed with settlers and some were gathered into government villages, before moving west into Mato Grosso. They resisted contact with Brazilian society until the 1940s and now live on reserves in eastern Mato Grosso.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xavante.jpg" alt="Xavante" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clockwise from top left: Roosewelt Pinheiro, Márcio Meira, Márcio Meira, Elza Fiúza, Roosewelt Pinheiro, Valter Campanato (all ABr … · CC BY 3.0 br · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xavante.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Xavante, also called Shavante, Chavante, Akuen, Akwe, Awen or Akwen, number about 30,000 people in eastern Mato Grosso and speak the Xavante language of the Jê family. A genetic study of 2015 found that the Xavante, like the Paiter-Surui and Karitiana, have ancestry partly related to Indigenous peoples of the Andaman Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Scientists speculate that this goes back to an earlier East Asian population, called Population Y, from which both lines diverged 15,000 to 30,000 years ago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Xavante were enslaved in the 18th century and afterwards tried to avoid contact. A period of coexistence with Brazilian settler society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> in the 19th century ended with their withdrawal to Mato Grosso between 1830 and 1860. Outsiders spoke of their rediscovery in the 1930s, and from 1946 to 1957 they were brought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>'s National Integration Program, though they still suffered massacres and disease. This history left a lasting distrust of outsiders, whom the Xavante call waradzu.</p><p>The Xavante leader Mário Juruna became the first Indigenous Brazilian to sit as a federal representative. In 1996 the Brazilian band Sepultura stayed and recorded with the Xavante for its album Roots, and some Xavante travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> in 1998 to join the band's Barulho Contra Fome (Noise Against Hunger) concert.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Xavante society is divided into two clans, the Âwawẽ and the Po'reza'õno, and marriage within one's own clan is not allowed. The two clans meet in traditional log races, in which teams carry palm trunks weighing as much as 80 kg to a set point. Young men pass through elaborate initiation rites: at about fourteen small wooden sticks are inserted in their earlobes, and the ornaments are enlarged over the rest of their lives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xavante” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conflicts with indigenous peoples such as the Kayapó and <b>Xavante</b> were frequent, and the government founded aldeias to settle them.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-goias">Captaincy of Goiás</a></li></ul></section>
